  "notes": "The prayer that sets this sacrifice beside those of Abel, Abraham and Melchisedech — the only place in the Roman Mass where the Old Testament sacrifices are named. SÍCUTI at w013 is accented on the antepenult: sicut plus the enclitic i, whose u is short, so the stress cannot fall on it; witness do accents the penult, adjudicated in the apparatus. ABEL at w022 and MELCHÍSEDECH at w036 are indeclinable and carry no declension; ÁBRAHÆ at w027 has a Latin genitive but a declension the corpus does not label. Whitaker's Words knows none of the three names; Collatinus knows all three, and the provenance of those tokens records it. QUOD at w031 is a relative with no expressed antecedent — that which Melchisedech offered — and its two appositions (sacrifícium, hóstiam) close the sentence.",
